{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1114208-starting-a-mage-chronicle-with-powerful-players","id":1114208,"title":"Starting a Mage Chronicle with powerful players","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-07-10T10:44:34-07:00","id":1114208,"title":"Starting a Mage Chronicle with powerful players","content":"Hi,<br /> <br /> I just finished my first chronicle and my players are eager to start a new one. Since they played for 2 years with the same characters, they want to start with new ones to try other paradigms. In this chronicle, they started with nothing, Awakening alone in the middle of New York. They were reached by a group of Mages who taught them Magick and created all together a Chantry. At the end of my chronicle, they reached Arete 4.<br /> <br /> I would like to start things differently, giving them now the role of the mentors with a well established Chantry or at least a lot of experience, may be giving them enough points to start at Arete 5. How would you handle the character creation ? Would you just give them a lot of experience points to spend how they see fit ? Or, would you just fix different starting creation points (in each category) ?<br /> <br /> How would you use a &quot;Apprentice&quot; kind of background trait ? I have one of my players wanting to have an apprentice but I'm not sure if I just role-play the thing or make him buy it with experience points. If I do that, what advantage can I give him ?<br /> <br /> For you to know, this new chronicle will probably start just before the Avatar Storm or the destruction of Doissetep. The point will be to play a political chronicle in the Revised metaplot. I want to begin just before to make them feel what the Avatar Storm cost them. None of the players know the Mage timeline at this point (since I played before the Avatar Storm in the first chronicle and they're new to the game).<br /> <br /> Thank you for your answer !","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8572-nicotheduck","name":"NicoTheDuck","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"364"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-10T11:05:36-07:00","id":1114215,"title":"","content":"There are rules to create powerful mages...but I suggest to ignore them (as they're terrible) and just give a lot of xp or freebies and let the playes expend it. Set a few &quot;rules&quot;, like having to buy at least one Sphere at x level to be considered Adept/Master. Only this time, it may be justified to expend xp in Backgrounds(new level x 2, or something across these lines).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How would you use a &quot;Apprentice&quot; kind of background trait ? I have one of my players wanting to have an apprentice but I'm not sure if I just role-play the thing or make him buy it with experience points. If I do that, what advantage can I give him ? </div> </div> </div>An apprentice may be an Ally...If it's helpful.<br /> <br /> In the Guide to the Traditions there were &quot;adversarial backgrounds&quot; (a sort of harmful backgrounds that worked like Flaws), and one was about having a particularly unhelpful apprentice. The kind that's always sequestered, messing your sanctum, summoning things she can't control...ya know.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 07-10-2017, 02:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-10T11:57:17-07:00","id":1114230,"title":"","content":"Ah ah ! I like that idea of Adversarial backgrounds <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> I would play it like a Flaw though, but this is funny ^^<br /> <br /> My problem with the freebies or XP is that my players are great role-playing kind of players during the sessions. But when experience points are concerned, they're machines ^^ So... I kind of fear they will spend them all in Spheres and will not let them do that to trap them later. To avoid that, I will set limits I think.<br /> <br /> What do you think of using the optional rule of &quot;the number of dots in a skill to help performing an Effect should at least equal the rank of the highest Sphere involved in the Effect&quot;, but applied to the character creation only. It strongly depends on the paradigm and this can help me balance a little the whole thing. I usually do not use that rule, being to strict for me. But in that particular case, I could summon it during character creation...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8572-nicotheduck","name":"NicoTheDuck","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"364"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-10T12:24:49-07:00","id":1114237,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>NicoTheDuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1114208-starting-a-mage-chronicle-with-powerful-players?p=1114230#post1114230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What do you think of using the optional rule of &quot;the number of dots in a skill to help performing an Effect should at least equal the rank of the highest Sphere involved in the Effect&quot;, but applied to the character creation only. It strongly depends on the paradigm and this can help me balance a little the whole thing. I usually do not use that rule, being to strict for me. But in that particular case, I could summon it during character creation...</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't really like the idea that you can't do master level magic without being world class at something mundane as well.<br /> <br /> I'd say if you wanted to limit the Spheres, tell them they can only be master of 1 sphere, and adept of no more than two. Then give them 16 dots to place in the spheres and don't allow them to buy more than that, but give them the option to sell any of their sphere dots for the appropriate number of freebie points for other stuff.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-10T12:49:37-07:00","id":1114245,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>NicoTheDuck</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">My problem with the freebies or XP is that my players are great role-playing kind of players during the sessions. But when experience points are concerned, they're machines ^^ So... I kind of fear they will spend them all in Spheres and will not let them do that to trap them later. To avoid that, I will set limits I think.<br /> <br /> What do you think of using the optional rule of &quot;the number of dots in a skill to help performing an Effect should at least equal the rank of the highest Sphere involved in the Effect&quot;, but applied to the character creation only. It strongly depends on the paradigm and this can help me balance a little the whole thing. I usually do not use that rule, being to strict for me. But in that particular case, I could summon it during character creation...</div> </div> </div> </div>If you're going to take that route you may want to reconsider to, as yourself stated, &quot;fix different starting creation points&quot;. At this point I think it's more or less the same, you want your players to choose backgrounds, skills and atributes. If you adjust the starting creation points, they will have to buy where you want them to buy, any other strategy can be avoided, say, by choosing Instruments that do not rely on a Skill, or that rely in only one.<br /> <br /> As an idea, you could make a few different &quot;templates&quot; with a varying amount of creation points, for your players to choose. For instance:<ul><li>&quot;The Experienced Wizard&quot;: Has a balanced set of creation points as appropiate for a strong member of the Traditions/Technocracy</li> <li>&quot;the Wise Hermit&quot;: Has little Backgrounds thanks to a cloistered life but an extra Sphere dot representing a focused live of introspection and magick</li> <li>&quot;The Illuminati&quot;: Has a lot more Backgrounds but a Sphere point less. Additionaly, the Illuminati can start with certain Backgrounds, like Resources, above 5 to represent mastery of the mortal world.</li> </ul>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 07-10-2017, 03:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-11T05:31:07-07:00","id":1114539,"title":"","content":"Kudos to you. Master level play can be insane. Power levels in Mage aren't linear at all. Morel like the inverse log kind of exponential and 5 dots in a Sphere is much higher on that line reaching up to infinity (Arete 6?. 6+?) than 4. And 4 is a LOT higher than 3, 2, 1 clustered down near the vertex.<br /> <br /> I've always run Adversarial Backgrounds EXACTLY like Flaws (there are even 100% 1-to-1 correspondences between several of them). I'd run an Apprentice in which time must be invested as per the Apprentice Background, but the amount of care and feeding would be directly related to the Freebies gained. It would be a high dot Apprentice who would be actively messing up your Sanctum through their incompetence. An Apprentice who has graduated might be a mid-range Ally. One that should be graduated or is really more of a Journeyperson still hanging around might be a high dot Retainer. <br /> <br /> Since you're worried about your players min-maxing if the get a glut of XP to spend, then don't give them that. The best way to do that is to allocate where the XP would go by using an advanced version of the normal character creation system. That's what that does. <br /> <br /> I dunno, but some raw numbers might be something like:<br /> <b>Attributes</b>: 8/6/4<br /> <b>Abilities</b>: 19/13/7<br /> <b>Arete</b>: 5 (cannot be raised by use of Freebies, because.... then it's a whole other game and and you have to face some questions outright, like Arete 6 = infiinte power or not....)<br /> <b>Spheres</b>: 12 dots, (I dunno, but you might want to break this down further. I probably would. If you are looking for the &quot;5 dot Sphere&quot; experience, maybe require one Sphere to be 5 dots and one of their Sect Specialty Spheres be 3 dots, if they don't choose to have a Specialty Sphere at 5. In any case, I would probably say that a Specialty Sphere needs to be at 3). <br /> <b>Willpower</b>: 5 (STRONGLY urge them to buy it up if you are using the &quot;can't use a Sphere unless you've got that many temp WP&quot; and even more strongly if you are using HDYDT-style Mind effects, unless they want their adversaries and possibly fellows having an open door policy with their brains)<br /> <b>Backgrounds</b>: 10 (and figure out how high you want to let them go with Backgrounds 6+)(If you are using the Chantry idea, you might want to throw them as many additional points as you are requiring them to spend).<br /> <b>Freebies</b>: 28<br /> <b>Flaws</b>: 10 (And maybe up to 5 extra for Echoes? To represent the kind of permanent Resonance a Master would have picked up)<br /> <br /> YMMV on the above, but I was slanting it to the higher end of power in regard to how mage characters normally start out, based on your premise that this is the game where they get to bring out the big guns.<br /> <br /> One idea might be to REQUIRE them to sink points into a mutual Chantry as a Shared Background. Each player would be able to say what part of the Chantry's constituent Backgrounds their expense indicates (which tells you what role they play in it). This might be particularly great for min-max players, you toss in the codicil that the real political power/leadership role (whether it's overtly acknowledged or not) is dependent on who puts the most points in. You might get a bidding war Amber DRPG-style to determine who the top dog the Chantry is. And such a bidding war is a great way to simulate the history of the characters in the Chantry and create some internal tensions within the group. Masters are like Elder vampires. Even if they are ostensibly still a cabal, with that kind of power comes individual interests that don't always line up...<br /> <br /> As a general note: Remember that the <i>players </i>will probably be fairly inept at using their Spheres. There is a HUGE difference in the level of ability and knowledge a player will have if they have to slog their way up than if they are given super-powers outright. They'll probably end up having some tricks they learned getting to Adept in the other game and will be having fun with the new mega-power of 5 dots Spheres, but, since they didn't really earn the power as players, they will be somewhat inept compared to what such characters. Keep that in mind when creating whatever adversarial situations they are facing. TBH, this is actually a problem with mage characters as is. They pretty much start midway along the path, as opposed to how Vamps and Werewolf starting players do and the players don't know what their character's Spheres can do compared to the intimate knowledge gained if you have to start out at Initiate levels or in an &quot;Awakenings&quot; game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-12T09:53:40-07:00","id":1115125,"title":"","content":"Thank you all for your answers. I think I'll try to define new numbers of points for each category, may be using some of what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1062_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1062\" data-vbnamecard=\"1062\">Ajax</a> suggested. I need to put it to test to see where it can lead.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8572-nicotheduck","name":"NicoTheDuck","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"364"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-15T11:51:03-07:00","id":1116495,"title":"","content":"4 in a sphere is much more powerful than 3 in a sphere. Rolling more dice for magic, which one does with higher Arete, is more likely to allow more powerful effects. Limiting spheres and Arete is the most significant way to control mage power.<br /> <br /> If you are having mages be masters of the chantry, they could be adepts with rank 4 in a sphere and Arete 5. If the PCs are teaching and competent, they should have more abilities than a starting PC, so I'd go with the scheme Ajax used and limit the top sphere to 4.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T01:56:38-07:00","id":1117642,"title":"","content":"I've been tinkering with a system for well rounded, powerful characters. My initial suggestion would be something like this.<br /> You divide the 9 spheres into 3 tiers of interest, choose 3 spheres to be your tier 1 spheres, 3 as tier 2 and 3 as tier 3.<br /> <br /> You cannot progress to the 4th dot in any of your tier 1 spheres until you have 3 in all your tier 1 spheres and at least 2 in all your tier 2 spheres. Tier 1s doesn't have to have any rank in them. If you want the 5th dot, you have to have the other tier 1 spheres at 4, tier 2s at 3 and tier 1s at 1.<br /> <br /> For example you have a member of the Order of Hermes. His tier 1 spheres are Forces, Mind and Prime, his tier 2 Spheres are Life, Matter and Spirit and lastly his tier 3 spheres are Correspondence, Entropy and Time.<br /> <br /> If he wants to have the 4th dot in Forces, he needs to raised Prime and Mind to 3, Life, Matter and Spirit at 2.<br /> When he wants to buy the 5th dot of Forces, Mind or Prime, he has to have sufficient mastery (4th dot) in the other two, his tier 2s must have advanced to the 3rd rank and the rest to at least 1.<br /> <br /> This leaves a well rounded yet, extremely powerful character and it also makes sure that the players doesn't rush to the 5th dot in one sphere, while leaving the others at 1 or even 0. It also makes sure that it takes quite a while before the characters can wreck the game with spheres at 4 or 5.<br /> <br /> Now sure, this system makes it impossible for a character to be a one trick pony, but in my opinion thats a good thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imageshack.com/i/j5vvlqp\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/j5vvlqp\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/ghw20supporterhiddengaroup\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/n18segp\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0v25ewp\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#404040\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">English is not my native language, so i apologize for errors in grammar or spelling.</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/392-cadmiumcadamium","name":"Cadmiumcadamium","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar392_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"936"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T09:37:41-07:00","id":1117806,"title":"","content":"Seems really complicated and not entirely necessary for some concepts. <br /> <br /> Characters don't have to be well-rounded at all. They can be very lop-sided and interesting in specfic things to the exclusion of all else.<br /> <br /> I could easily see, say, a Hermetic Flambeau mage who has Forces 5, Prime 5 and Entropy 2. (Using the &quot;you get 12 dots for Spheres&quot; from my system above). That's completely outside that system, but it's a very viable character concept. &quot;I blow things up. I blow them up good. I can blow them up with finesse. I can blow them up big time. I can blow them up so bad it's like they never existed.&quot; Or &quot;Power, power, power, it's all about the power&quot; (though Entropy 2 could be something else in that case) Such a character concept would be needless burdened by having to try to allocate this over here and that over there. It probably wouldn't be possible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T21:24:56-07:00","id":1118062,"title":"","content":"While well rounded sphere levels are definitely powerful they are rarely feasible without a LOT of xp, and game time. Specialization is therefore the (only) way for most. At least after 3 dots.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/987-tuch","name":"Tuch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar987_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-19T03:36:17-07:00","id":1118123,"title":"","content":"I find the suggested limits proposed by <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/392-cadmiumcadamium\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar392_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"392\" data-vbnamecard=\"392\">Cadmiumcadamium</a> a bit to harash, but I like the principle. Ultra specialization seems a lot like minmaxing... I have the impression that Mages are much more diversified (if it's the word). Even if they do not need to have many dots on all Spheres, many Spheres are used as support Spheres for their specialization, like Correspondance, Like, Matter or Time. Your Hermetic Flambeau Mage will probably use Correspondance too. It seems unlikely he can raise storms and cyclones and be enable to see across some distance through flames or drain energy from Nodes also across some distance. Mastery often comes with global knowledge don't you think ? Ultra specialization comes late. A study begins with general things. Harry Potter is a master at blowing things up, but he ulso had to learn a bit about potions and herboristery and enchantments (transmutations, healing, etc. even if he's pretty bad at it).<br /> <br /> Not sure I'm clear on the matter though...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8572-nicotheduck","name":"NicoTheDuck","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"364"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-19T04:11:50-07:00","id":1118129,"title":"","content":"I agree, If you see official character sheets, you will notice that Spheres are usually spread, not always, but almost always. Mages are supposed to be generalistic, even if rules wise this doesn't make a lot of sense (unless ST it's really hard with the Seekings).<br /> <br /> However, for the same reason, disalowing Spheres at 0 just 'cause your mage it's strong doesn't make a lot of sense. Look at that same character sheets that have a spread of Spheres at low levels, and you will see most mages do have Spheres at 0. Inhability with one or more Spheres isn't rare at all. I also think the suggested limits are harsh.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-19T05:11:34-07:00","id":1118134,"title":"","content":"Yep, that's right. I never said that a powerful Mage would not have Spheres at 0. Just saying that only three spheres at 5/5/4 is not realistic neither. It should be more balanced. But the Hermetic Flambeau Mage could have become powerful without learning the Spirit Sphere for exemple (and even there, Harry Potter has the mastery of the Patronus, spirit of happy days... <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8572-nicotheduck","name":"NicoTheDuck","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"364"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-19T10:02:52-07:00","id":1118201,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>NicoTheDuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1114208-starting-a-mage-chronicle-with-powerful-players?p=1118123#post1118123\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I find the suggested limits proposed by <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/392-cadmiumcadamium\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar392_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"392\" data-vbnamecard=\"392\">Cadmiumcadamium</a> a bit to harash, but I like the principle. Ultra specialization seems a lot like minmaxing... I have the impression that Mages are much more diversified (if it's the word). Even if they do not need to have many dots on all Spheres, many Spheres are used as support Spheres for their specialization, like Correspondance, Like, Matter or Time. Your Hermetic Flambeau Mage will probably use Correspondance too. It seems unlikely he can raise storms and cyclones and be enable to see across some distance through flames or drain energy from Nodes also across some distance. Mastery often comes with global knowledge don't you think ? Ultra specialization comes late. A study begins with general things. Harry Potter is a master at blowing things up, but he ulso had to learn a bit about potions and herboristery and enchantments (transmutations, healing, etc. even if he's pretty bad at it).<br /> <br /> Not sure I'm clear on the matter though...</div> </div> </div> </div>He doesn't have to throw storms with Correspondence 5. He is willing, nay, exalted, by standing at Ground Zero, the beating heart of a fiery whirlwind of destruction an chaos. His view of magic is primal and visceral. Hands on. And he is very very VERY good at what he does.<br /> <br /> And why isn't that a perfectly okay character concept?<br /> <br /> It's not even, essentially, mine. It's derived from the character ideas used to describe different paths to power by a couple of mages back in Ars Magica, 2nd ed. The first is a Criamon (alas, poor Criamon), who is very good with spontaneous magic, has a broad base from which to work but doesn't have anything really to earth shattering to hand. The other is a Flambeau mage who pours his efforts into Pilum of Fire (a Rote by any other name, but what Mage calls &quot;Static&quot; and &quot;Dynamic&quot; magic were much more integrated into a single system back then) and is VERY good at shooting out fire and burning the crap out of things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1062,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","user":"Ajax","date":". 07-19-2017, 01:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-19T10:28:04-07:00","id":1118207,"title":"","content":"I don't think it's bad. It's somewhat unorthodox. You could be asked, if you want to do a Flambeau mage who pours all his efforts into Pilum of Fire, or Creo Ignem, why don't you play a Sorcerer instead, same with storms. As you yourself say, &quot;Static&quot; and &quot;Dynamic&quot; aren't integrated in Mage system. Mages are supposed to be &quot;Dynamical&quot;...<br /> <br /> But the concept shouldn't be forbidden.<br /> <br /> We have rules to create an awakened mage with fangs that sucks blood, a mage that's veeeery focused shouldn't be out of the question.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-19T11:03:43-07:00","id":1118217,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1114208-starting-a-mage-chronicle-with-powerful-players?p=1118207#post1118207\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think it's bad. It's somewhat unorthodox. You could be asked, if you want to do a Flambeau mage who pours all his efforts into Pilum of Fire, or Creo Ignem, why don't you play a Sorcerer instead, same with storms. As you yourself say, &quot;Static&quot; and &quot;Dynamic&quot; aren't integrated in Mage system. Mages are supposed to be &quot;Dynamical&quot;...<br /> <br /> But the concept shouldn't be forbidden.<br /> <br /> We have rules to create an awakened mage with fangs that sucks blood, a mage that's veeeery focused shouldn't be out of the question.</div> </div> </div> </div>It was the idea that carried through. In Ars Magica, it was considered totally okay to make a generalist mage who wasn't great at big spells but was very versatile. And it was okay to make up a mage who wanted to be good at a particular thing to exclusion of everything else. The system under discussion privileges one of these to the exclusion of the other, and, in fact, to want to be specialized was at one point called &quot;power gaming&quot;. Which, it may be, but, it's in the sense of getting a LOT of this restricted types of power at the expense of generalism. And it's not &quot;being a power gamer&quot; to want to be the one and not the other. if that's not the character you want to play. <br /> <br /> And, since Sorcerers and Static Magic haven't been addressed head on in M20, especially and particularly when dealing with 1) PCs and 2) issues of having high levels of power, and the past history has been all over the place, I'm not poking that pile of steaming deer guts with a stick. I like this stick and I don't want Sorcerer goo all over it. However, in Ars Magica that was it was entirely possible to be both a static and a dynamic mage at the same time in a way similar to, but more Ascension-like than Awakenings &quot;Real&quot; Rotes and Arcana are/were. The Flambeau probably had some skill in Creo and Ignam, even if the majority of his ooomph was going to Pilum of Fire.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-19T12:49:42-07:00","id":1118249,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">It was the idea that carried through. In Ars Magica, it was considered totally okay to make a generalist mage who wasn't great at big spells but was very versatile. And it was okay to make up a mage who wanted to be good at a particular thing to exclusion of everything else. The system under discussion privileges one of these to the exclusion of the other, and, in fact, to want to be specialized was at one point called &quot;power gaming&quot;. Which, it may be, but, it's in the sense of getting a LOT of this restricted types of power at the expense of generalism. And it's not &quot;being a power gamer&quot; to want to be the one and not the other. if that's not the character you want to play.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I think it's debatable if it's powergaming or not (Isn't it always).<br /> <br /> The main argument to call it powergaming it's the power gap between 5th/4th levels and 3 or less. Also, getting high levels it's substantially more costly in Experience than getting low levels. Thus, you could be doing this knowing that getting other relevant spheres at relevant levels will cost you less in the long run.<br /> <br /> This idea, actually, it's part of how Mage it's build. Think about these things:<br /> <br /> *All games forbide to get Skills to 5 without expending freebies.<br /> <br /> *Mage it's one of the few games that utterly forbbides you to get more than 3 in Spheres at the begining (and, now that we're at it, you NEED to have at least 2 Spheres. Even your system impedes to get less than 3 Spheres for the character. Does that sound like a complete specialist for you?, you can't imagine even stricter, more focused, concepts?)<br /> <br /> In general, getting things maxed smells like a powergamer...<br /> <br /> But I can powergame without getting 2 things maxed. Arguably certain Sphere combos at 4-3 could be more scary than having just 2 Spheres at 5<br /> <br /> <i>What's the difference between a powergamer build and one that isn't powergamer?.</i><br /> <br /> I think &quot;powergaming&quot; it's an attitude towards the rules, It has nothing to do with the validity of your concept.<br /> <br /> Some rules may be less conductive to PG than others, and, to me, this is a desirable cuality...but, of course, it's not the be all and end all (I wouldn't like Mage at all if this was the case <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And, since Sorcerers and Static Magic haven't been addressed head on in M20, especially and particularly when dealing with 1) PCs and 2) issues of having high levels of power, and the past history has been all over the place, I'm not poking that pile of steaming deer guts with a stick. I like this stick and I don't want Sorcerer goo all over it. However, in Ars Magica that was it was entirely possible to be both a static and a dynamic mage at the same time in a way similar to, but more Ascension-like than Awakenings &quot;Real&quot; Rotes and Arcana are/were. The Flambeau probably had some skill in Creo and Ignam, even if the majority of his ooomph was going to Pilum of Fire. </div> </div> </div>I think someone could say:<br /> <br /> &quot;I don't know about Ars and don't fell compelled to cater to the interest of players of a game that had a forgoten connection with my game in prehistory, \u200bMages do not have Creo Ignem, much less a stranding Rote unconected to Spheres. That's not how magick works&quot; .<br /> <br /> OR<br /> <br /> &quot;I'm not poking that pile of steaming deer guts with a stick. I like this stick and I don't want Ars Magica goo all over it.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 07-19-2017, 04:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-19T13:19:46-07:00","id":1118260,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1114208-starting-a-mage-chronicle-with-powerful-players?p=1118249#post1118249\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think someone could say:<br /> <br /> &quot;I don't know about Ars and don't fell compelled to cater to the interest of players of a game that had a forgoten connection with my game in prehistory, \u200bMages do not have Creo Ignem, much less a stranding Rote unconected to Spheres. That's not how magick works&quot; .<br /> <br /> OR<br /> <br /> &quot;I'm not poking that pile of steaming deer guts with a stick. I like this stick and I don't want Ars Magica goo all over it.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>So...... we aren't supposed to ever mention other games with relevant aspects that might relate to the subject at hand? There are lessons in D&amp;D that are very relevant to aspects of Mage that are frequently both ignored and problematic. And Ars Magica had a VERY important role in the founding of 1) ALL of White Wolf games, as they affected the fundamentals of Vampire which means the fundamentals of the WOD Engine, and 2) Mage specifically, as concepts of both fluff and crunch were lifted either in whole or in part.<br /> <br /> Here is baby. Here is bathwater. You know what to do.<br /> <br /> But, in any case, to the matter at hand. Building a high end game isn't the same as a building a normal level Mage game with XP added on. Nor is it the same as playing an Initiate game with XP to bring it up to Mage's default start and then up to a Master's level. If you build characters in each of those regimes they will end up being very different beasts. And the actual powergaming and min-maxing is more likely to come in to play with the latter two. Characters who are purpose built aren't going to look organic at all. And the first system might not make characters that look like characters who were created as per normal then played up to Master, but Master-level games that start that way are a different kettle of fish than Master games from the get go.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-19T17:46:37-07:00","id":1118324,"title":"","content":"You're right about the powergaming aspect. Any system will have it's powergamers. It's always upon the ST to detect that particular behavior. Some rules may help a little, but in the end it doesn't matter.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So...... we aren't supposed to ever mention other games with relevant aspects that might relate to the subject at hand? There are lessons in D&amp;D that are very relevant to aspects of Mage that are frequently both ignored and problematic. And Ars Magica had a VERY important role in the founding of 1) ALL of White Wolf games, as they affected the fundamentals of Vampire which means the fundamentals of the WOD Engine, and 2) Mage specifically, as concepts of both fluff and crunch were lifted either in whole or in part. </div> </div> </div>I'm not saying that, I was just extrapolating what you said before about Sorcerer. Sorcerer it's &quot;other game&quot;. One that is related to Mage because all of Sorcerer books come from Mage books, even when Sorcerers aren't mentioned in ANY Basic (I'm not sure how they not being in M20 changes anything). It's every bit as relevant as Ars Magica, perhaps even more (as Sorcerer was written with Mage concepts in mind), to determine if a concept falls under Dynamic or it's better as Static magic.<br /> <br /> Now I'm not saying that your concept <i>has </i>to be a Sorcerer. I'm saying that most mages<i> ARE supposed to be pretty Dynamical</i>.<br /> <br /> For instance, on of the good sections of Masters of the Art even suggest that almost no Archmage has Spheres at 0, only to blow up that idea with it's horrid character gen system that makes nigh impossible to not have a sphere at 0.<br /> Spheres are supposed to be connected, that's one of the main differences between Ascencion and Awakening, in Awakening Spheres are stand alone and you don't need platonic Prime to &quot;blow things up&quot; with Forces. I'm not sure how removing the dust from Ars Magica's connection can change what Mage concepts are fringe or not.<br /> <br /> I think it would good if Ascencion rules followed the fluff and <i>suggested </i>a spread (but I'm not ok with <i>forcing </i>it). Cadmiumcadamium's system ends being evil because it excludes many concepts (including your specialist Ars Magica mage), but the spirit behind the system isn't evil.<br /> <br /> To propose another, starting from yours: You could give one sphere at 5 and one at 3 <i><b>for free</b></i>,and then limmit the rest to 3 unless the player expends freebies to buy extra points. That way you can have a &quot;specialist&quot;, you only need to spend 14 freebies, but it suggests to a wider spread. 12 points are too few to spread along the 9 Spheres and still be a Master (with freebies you can cover them all, barely, you will still look like a &quot;specialist&quot; with your big one at 5 and nearly every other at 1-2), this is why MotA system it's evil, but with 20 (24?) you have enough dots to be a generalist, and the constraints are there to push you to the levels 1-3 instead of starting with all at 4-5 (without taking away the option).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 07-19-2017, 08:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-20T06:18:28-07:00","id":1118504,"title":"","content":"Altough it makes a huge difference if you get for example 100 xp at once instead of slowly accumulated 100 xp, I can recommend to use the system from Ars Magica and convert most or all of the chargen points to XP.<br /> <br /> At the very least go and ditch freebies for thrice as many XP.<br /> <br /> And you have chargen which allows a specialist (costly, meaning fewer dots overall) and a generalist (more dots, but more spread out).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>So, this Zen Master walks up to a hot dog stand and says: &quot;Make me one with everything!&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12022-nonsense","name":"Nonsense","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12022_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"815"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-20T08:30:09-07:00","id":1118574,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1114208-starting-a-mage-chronicle-with-powerful-players?p=1118324#post1118324\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I'm not saying that, I was just extrapolating what you said before about Sorcerer. Sorcerer it's &quot;other game&quot;. One that is related to Mage because all of Sorcerer books come from Mage books, even when Sorcerers aren't mentioned in ANY Basic (I'm not sure how they not being in M20 changes anything). It's every bit as relevant as Ars Magica, perhaps even more (as Sorcerer was written with Mage concepts in mind), to determine if a concept falls under Dynamic or it's better as Static magic.<br /> <br /> Now I'm not saying that your concept <i>has </i>to be a Sorcerer. I'm saying that most mages<i> ARE supposed to be pretty Dynamical</i>.<br /> <br /> For instance, on of the good sections of Masters of the Art even suggest that almost no Archmage has Spheres at 0, only to blow up that idea with it's horrid character gen system that makes nigh impossible to not have a sphere at 0.<br /> Spheres are supposed to be connected, that's one of the main differences between Ascencion and Awakening, in Awakening Spheres are stand alone and you don't need platonic Prime to &quot;blow things up&quot; with Forces. I'm not sure how removing the dust from Ars Magica's connection can change what Mage concepts are fringe or not.<br /> <br /> I think it would good if Ascencion rules followed the fluff and <i>suggested </i>a spread (but I'm not ok with <i>forcing </i>it). Cadmiumcadamium's system ends being evil because it excludes many concepts (including your specialist Ars Magica mage), but the spirit behind the system isn't evil.<br /> <br /> To propose another, starting from yours: You could give one sphere at 5 and one at 3 <i><b>for free</b></i>,and then limmit the rest to 3 unless the player expends freebies to buy extra points. That way you can have a &quot;specialist&quot;, you only need to spend 14 freebies, but it suggests to a wider spread. 12 points are too few to spread along the 9 Spheres and still be a Master (with freebies you can cover them all, barely, you will still look like a &quot;specialist&quot; with your big one at 5 and nearly every other at 1-2), this is why MotA system it's evil, but with 20 (24?) you have enough dots to be a generalist, and the constraints are there to push you to the levels 1-3 instead of starting with all at 4-5 (without taking away the option).</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, I see. To clarify: I just remembered the bit from Ars Magica as an example of different ways to use the system in place to focus or de-focus the character. The beginning Ars Magica Flambeau with his one big-ass spell and the Mage Flambeau Master wouldn't be very much alike in any other way that the specialization. Specialization doesn't mean not dynamic or creative, he's just being creative with fewer but more powerful and versatile building blocks. The Mage Master Flambeau wouldn't be working a few Rotes (or maybe he would, but who cares? Since Rotes are as close to meaningless a crunch concept as they can be in Ascension and still exist) he would have all the magnificent power of all different Forces at his beck and call and would be able to manipulate the fundamentals of magic in awe-inspiring ways, not just projecting one really big flame blast.<br /> <br /> As for the concept of not dividing Dynamic and Static magic into two mutually exclusive boxes and calling one &quot;Sorcery&quot; and the other &quot;Real Magic&quot;. That's an intrinsic problem that Mage has saddled itself with that causes no end of issues. It ends up in conflicted fluff and baroque existential hand-wringing crunch to try to manifest what doesn't really shake out as a viable concept. And it's inherently contradictory with some of the basic premises of the game from the start until now. For example, could you run a Sorcerer of any iteration in a Master level game like this? If not, why not? If you can, what are the issues between the playability of the Sorcerer vs. that of the Master Mage? And why, beyond some completely arbitrary decisions made back in the day and never really examined for their value, is this even an issue?<br /> <br /> See? I don't want to touch the Sorcerer pile of guts with this stick. It's a mess to begin with and there's particularly almost nothing in that pile that is easy to bring to bear on this specific issue.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-21T09:53:24-07:00","id":1119008,"title":"","content":"A Sorcerer wouldn't stand a chance in a game of Masters if power balance it's important. If balance isn't important then you may as well play with one. This has more to do with a ST style than with methaphysics or character concepts.<br /> <br /> I hope you see my point, however, Mage forces you to start with at least 2 Spheres, thus total specialization isn't even possible. YOUR system forces one to start with 3, something a true fan of specialists may resent (why you force me to buy levels that don't catter as much to my &quot;blow things up&quot; concept as Forces alone would?, why you insist that Entropy it's relevant? I'm fine with Prime 2! I don't want to put my points there for a bland excuse like &quot;you're so good at blowing things that you vanish them&quot; (which it's no different from &quot;you're soo good that you can blow things in different timelines(Time)/you blow them across various layers of reality(Spirit)&quot;))<br /> <br /> Mage isn't a game about &quot;specialists&quot;, you seemt to be demanding Mage to do things the way Ars do &quot;because Ars has this issue sovled so much better&quot;. Your concept isn't &quot;a perfectly okay character concept&quot; precisely because of that. When you need to go to Ars Magica to find a character concept, you shouold know you past a line.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-22T09:35:01-07:00","id":1119269,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1114208-starting-a-mage-chronicle-with-powerful-players?p=1119008#post1119008\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A Sorcerer wouldn't stand a chance in a game of Masters if power balance it's important. If balance isn't important then you may as well play with one. This has more to do with a ST style than with methaphysics or character concepts.<br /> <br /> I hope you see my point, however, Mage forces you to start with at least 2 Spheres, thus total specialization isn't even possible. YOUR system forces one to start with 3, something a true fan of specialists may resent (why you force me to buy levels that don't catter as much to my &quot;blow things up&quot; concept as Forces alone would?, why you insist that Entropy it's relevant? I'm fine with Prime 2! I don't want to put my points there for a bland excuse like &quot;you're so good at blowing things that you vanish them&quot; (which it's no different from &quot;you're soo good that you can blow things in different timelines(Time)/you blow them across various layers of reality(Spirit)&quot;))<br /> <br /> Mage isn't a game about &quot;specialists&quot;, you seemt to be demanding Mage to do things the way Ars do &quot;because Ars has this issue sovled so much better&quot;. Your concept isn't &quot;a perfectly okay character concept&quot; precisely because of that. When you need to go to Ars Magica to find a character concept, you shouold know you past a line.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay. I disagree. Mage can be about either or both and is probably about something somewhere in the middle. Saying &quot;You get 6 dots and you can only go up to 3, so you have to be a generalist&quot; is not really the point. I can have a very tight character concept and take 2 Spheres at 3 dots. Or I can be Mr. Looky-loo and take 6 different Spheres at 1. Or I can do 3 at 2. Or 3/2/1. Or 2/2/1/1. Or 2/1/1/1/1. Or add some with Freebies and Flaws. None of that means the game promotes a particular style. It doesn't. And it doesn't make the specialist weaker. There are creativity workarounds to many of the pitfalls of not having &quot;Sphere X&quot; or, even just not trying to do everything, particularly the things you are not good at (as in, &quot;Why I am a member of a cabal&quot;) A very very strong starting cabal concept could be 4 Mages with a 3/3 allocation of Spheres and no overlap (just ditch Entropy as everything it can do, some other Sphere can do as well and usually better). Sure, they won't be coordinating effects, but the game actually DOES discourage that with crunch, so it's not a great loss. <br /> <br /> In fact, in our game right now, most of the characters are specialists in a narrow range of things with a few generalists to round things out. We have a Forces 4/Prime 4 Hermetic who is also our Middle Umbra Specialist. A Mind/Time Cultist who has picked up Correspondence (so he's effectively everywhere). A Matter/Life Verbena. We're playtesting a different version of Data and a Matter Alternate Sphere &quot;Machine&quot; and we have one character with both of those. The party &quot;leader&quot; is a NWO is the generalist who overlaps with pretty much everyone else a bit and specializes in Entropy (which is slightly different in that is less of an overlap Sphere than it is about articulating and disarticulating relationships between different Spheres/aspects of reality than it is a hash of duplicating abilities from other Spheres in a herky-jerky stepwise fashion). We've added in a few new players who plug any gaps and play well with the specialists in their areas. And it works REALLY well. We've been playing this game for over two years now and are in the middle of the climatic ending.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}